<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Jamaican Maroons</h1><p class="st-meta">1655 CE – present (approx.) · capital Accompong</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Self-governing communities of escaped Africans in Jamaica's mountains who fought Britain to a standstill and won treaties of autonomy in 1739.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the English invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-jamaica">Spanish Jamaica</a> in 1655, many enslaved Africans fled into the interior and formed free communities. Their numbers grew as runaways from British plantations joined them, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a>. Two main groups emerged: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-maroons">Leeward Maroons</a> of the Cockpit Country and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-maroons">Windward Maroons</a> of the Blue Mountains.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Maroon towns were run by elected or chosen leaders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cudjoe">Cudjoe</a> in the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanny-of-the-maroons">Nanny</a> in the east, now a national heroine. They farmed hidden provision grounds, hunted wild hogs and used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics and abeng horn signals. After the First Maroon War, treaties of 1739 and 1740 granted them land and autonomy in return for returning runaways and helping suppress revolts, a clause that soured relations with the enslaved.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-maroon-war">the Second Maroon War</a> of 1795 to 1796 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trelawny-town">Trelawny Town</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> were deported to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. Other communities, at Accompong, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moore-town">Moore Town</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-town-jamaica">Charles Town</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotts-hall-jamaica">Scott's Hall</a>, survive and still claim treaty rights, keeping Kromanti ritual language and traditions.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jamaican-maroons.jpg" alt="Jamaican Maroons" width="320" height="481"><figcaption>Image: Abraham Raimbach (1776-1843) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonard_Parkinson,_Maroon_Leader,_Jamaica,_1796.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1655 CE – present (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Accompong</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cudjoe, Nanny of the Maroons, Quao</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>African traditional religion and Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Jamaican Creole, Kromanti</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/jamaica">Jamaica</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Windward Maroons, Leeward Maroons, Accompong</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=jamaican-maroons" aria-label="Jamaican Maroons on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2572 1501 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jamaican Maroons lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2892 1757L2917 1766"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2917 1766h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2892 1757h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2892 1757h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Accompong. <a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=jamaican-maroons">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

